## Getting Started

To start building **CincaiAndroid** (based on LineageOS), you’ll need to get familiar with [Source Control Tools](https://source.android.com/setup/develop).

---

## 🛠 Preparing Your Fedora

Before you can start cincai-ing your Android build, make sure Fedora is ready to rumble.

---

### 1. Install Build Essentials

You can do this the short cincai way:

```bash
sudo dnf install @c-development @development-tools

Or, for the more ceremonial Fedora fans:

sudo dnf group install "C Development Tools and Libraries" "Development Tools"

2. Install Additional Required Packages

These are not optional. Missing one = hours of head-scratching.

sudo dnf install --skip-unavailable \
android-tools \
ccache \
clang \
cmake \
git-lfs \
gperf \
lzop \
maven \
meson \
pngcrush \
rpm-build \
schedtool \
dpkg-dev \
libstdc++ \
ncurses-compat-libs \
python3-mako \
python3-networkx \
syslinux-devel \
openssl-devel-engine \
libxcrypt-compat

    ✅ --skip-unavailable is used just in case some packages aren't in your enabled repos.
    But ideally, keep your repos fresh.

3. Setup Your Workspace

mkdir -p ~/bin ~/android

4. Get the repo Tool

Download and install Google’s repo tool:

curl https://storage.googleapis.com/git-repo-downloads/repo > ~/bin/repo
chmod a+x ~/bin/repo

And make sure ~/bin is in your $PATH.
5. Repo Initialization

cd ~/android
repo init -u https://github.com/dmmartin/leandroid.git -b most-cincai --git-lfs

6. Sync the Source

repo sync

7. Build Time!

## ⚡ Performance Advice & Sanity Preservation Tips

If you’re blessed with a **Ryzen Threadripper** (or anything above 16 cores),  
**don’t be shy — use it**:

```bash
repo sync -c -j32

    ⏱ With proper I/O and a good connection, a full repo sync and source update can complete in under 30 minutes.

Compare this to some YouTubers (yes, we're looking at you Gary Explains) who thought syncing AOSP on a 2-core machine was a great tutorial idea.
He repo-synced all night… and built all day. 😩

Please don’t repeat his sins.
❌ Don’t blindly follow "Building for AOSP devices" tutorials.

Those devices are officially supported, with ready manifests and build guides written by Google engineers.
Of course it works. It’s basically copy-paste with coffee.
🛠 Building for Unsupported Devices?

If you’re trying to build for a non-AOSP or unlisted device, the game changes.

    💡 Tip: Keep ChatGPT open on the side.
    GPT has helped countless devs work around:

    Missing or broken device trees

    Proprietary blob incompatibility

    Manifest mismatches

    Weird extract-files.py fails

    Mysterious build errors

    🔧 Remember: Building AOSP is easy.
    Building for your own random device with no official trees, missing blobs, and weird Qualcomm configs — that's real Android development.

ChatGPT won’t judge you. But it will save you.

